WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:05.120
Every biological process can be traced
back to genes, and in many cases,

2
00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:09.280
specific diseases can be linked to
mistakes in genes. Understanding the

3
00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:13.900
exact role a gene plays in biology or
disease is challenging, however,

4
00:00:13.910 --> 00:00:19.450
because multicellular organisms like
humans are complex. Just over 30 years

5
00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:23.220
ago, a group of scientists made a
pivotal discovery that led to the

6
00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:27.310
development of an important tool that
changed the way scientists could study

7
00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:32.490
the role of a single gene and its
impact on an entire biological system:

8
00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:37.630
the knockout mouse. You can think of a
knockout mouse like baking a cake with

9
00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:42.320
an ingredient removed. Removing even
one ingredient can lead to a cake that

10
00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:47.650
doesn't rise properly or isn't sweet
enough. Similarly, taking away a single

11
00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:53.280
gene in an organism can reveal what key
ingredient that gene provides to making

12
00:00:53.290 --> 00:00:58.510
a fully functional organism. While the
theory is simple, the process of making

13
00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:03.410
a knockout mouse is complex and took
decades to develop. To make a knockout

14
00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:08.740
mouse, researchers first isolate
embryonic stem cells (ES cells) from an

15
00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:13.960
early mouse embryo, one of the earliest
developmental stages of any organism.

16
00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:19.220
Most ES cells are a type of cell that
still have the potential to develop

17
00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:24.360
into any cell or tissue in the body.
So, if a gene is removed from them then

18
00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:29.840
all subsequent cells and tissues will
also be devoid of the gene. One of the

19
00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:34.980
most common ways scientists can remove
a gene from ES cells is using a method

20
00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:40.680
called homologous recombination. In
this process, a piece of DNA containing

21
00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:45.460
identical genetic sequences to the
region surrounding the gene of interest

22
00:01:45.470 --> 00:01:50.700
is used to swap out genetic material in
the ES cell by either rendering the

23
00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:55.270
gene dysfunctional with the addition of
a mutation or replacing it with some

24
00:01:55.270 --> 00:02:00.500
DNA that interferes with the gene.
After the gene is removed, the ES cells

25
00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:05.670
are inserted into a mouse embryo and
implanted into the uterus of a female

26
00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:10.350
mouse. The resulting baby mice will
have some tissues where the desired

27
00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:15.970
gene is removed, those originating from
the ES cell, and others where it is

28
00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:21.200
not, those originating from the embryo
itself. These mice are known as

29
00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:26.840
chimeric mice, named after an ancient
greek beast, the chimera, consisting of

30
00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:31.350
body parts from several different
animals. To generate mice where the

31
00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:35.860
target gene is gone in all cells, the
partial knockout mouse is bred with a

32
00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:40.340
normal mouse. The resulting mouse
litter will have some mice where one

33
00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:45.310
copy of the target gene is removed,
also called heterozygotes. These

34
00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:50.370
heterozygotes are further bred together
over several generations to produce a

35
00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:55.000
line of mice where the desired gene is
completely removed, also known as

36
00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:00.750
homozygous knockouts. This discovery of
how to make a knockout mouse was so

37
00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:04.390
critical that the three scientists
involved won the Nobel Prize in

38
00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:10.480
Physiology or Medicine in 1997. Since
then, knockout mice have been critical

39
00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:15.010
contributors to numerous scientific
advances. Given humans and mice are

40
00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:20.400
identical in 85% of genes that make
proteins, studying a gene's role in a

41
00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:26.080
mouse can provide critical insights to
human biology and disease. One of the

42
00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:31.090
first examples was the creation of a
mouse model for cystic fibrosis, a

43
00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:36.850
disease caused by a mutation in the
CFTR gene. Knocking out this one gene

44
00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:41.730
in mice recapitulated many of the
cystic fibrosis symptoms observed in

45
00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:45.450
humans, allowing scientists to
understand the complexities of this

46
00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:50.780
disease, ultimately helping us treat
the same condition in humans. Since

47
00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:54.440
then, mouse models have advanced
research for numerous diseases

48
00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:59.520
including arthritis, various cancers
and Parkinson's disease. In recent

49
00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:04.250
years, knockout mouse technology has
evolved considerably, most recently

50
00:04:04.250 --> 00:04:09.300
with the advent of CRISPR-Cas9.
CRISPR-Cas9 is based on a naturally

51
00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:14.300
occurring immune system in bacteria to
target and change specific genetic

52
00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:14.960
locations.

53
00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:21.110
This method provides several benefits.
One, it can edit genes within an embryo

54
00:04:21.110 --> 00:04:27.050
itself, which can bypass the need for
ES cells. This also allows editing in

55
00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:31.020
animal models other than mice, as ES
cells are not available for most

56
00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:36.070
animals. Two, it is extremely precise
and can edit multiple genetic locations

57
00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:40.940
simultaneously. And three, it can
create a knockout mouse in about six

58
00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:45.450
months, compared to the nearly two
years more traditional methods require.

59
00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:51.290
As with any scientific method, knockout
mice also have certain drawbacks. Some

60
00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:55.690
genes, for example, are so essential
that removing them leads to the death

61
00:04:55.690 --> 00:05:00.920
of the embryo or a mouse that cannot
fully develop. Additionally, many

62
00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:05.710
biological processes or diseases are
impacted or driven by more than one

63
00:05:05.710 --> 00:05:10.800
gene, so removing a single one might
not replicate all disease symptoms.

64
00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:15.510
Lastly, there are important biological
and genetic differences between mice

65
00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:20.360
and humans, so observations made in
mice may not always translate to humans.

66
00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:24.220
Regardless, from the time they were
first introduced, knockout mice have

67
00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:28.960
become an indispensable part of many
biological laboratories around the

68
00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:34.190
world, contributing to nearly every
scientific medical advance. Now,

69
00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:39.110
knockout technology is used in many
different types of model organisms, for

70
00:05:39.110 --> 00:05:43.520
instance, flies, worms and fish,
further revolutionizing scientific

71
00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:44.160
research.

